'''HMS ''Hyperion''''' was a 32-gun [[frigate]] of the [[Royal Navy]]. Copied from the design of the French frigate [[French frigate Magicienne (1778)|''Magicienne'']], she was [[ship commissioning|commissioned]] in 1808. Under the command of [[Thomas Brodie (Royal Navy officer)|Thomas Brodie]], she served in the [[Mediterranean Sea]] for two years before moving to the [[Jamaica Station (Royal Navy)|Jamaica Station]]. In February 1811, an incident at [[Gonaïves]] resulted in Haitians killing three members of ''Hyperion''{{'}}s crew, and Brodie died of an illness probably caught there. Now commanded by [[William Pryce Cumby]], ''Hyperion'' served on the [[Newfoundland Station]] on fishery protection duties in 1812, returning in the following year to serve in the [[English Channel]] and [[Bay of Biscay]], where she captured the American [[privateer]] ''Rattlesnake'' on 3 June 1814.

Recommissioned after the end of the [[Napoleonic Wars]], ''Hyperion'' briefly served off [[Portsmouth]] as an escort to [[George IV|George, Prince Regent]], before she was transferred to the [[South America Station]] in 1820 to protect trade impacted by the [[Chilean War of Independence]]. ''Hyperion'' was present at [[Callao]] when the [[Capture of the frigate Esmeralda|Spanish frigate ''Esmeralda'' was captured by Chile]] on 5 November. ''Hyperion''{{'}}s captain, [[Thomas Searle]], fostered such a negative relationship with the Chilean naval commander [[Thomas Cochrane, 10th Earl of Dundonald|Lord Cochrane]] that soon afterwards the frigate was ordered back to Britain. Under [[James Lillicrap]] ''Hyperion'' then joined the [[Cape of Good Hope Station]] in 1821, moving to combat piracy off [[Cuba]] in the following year. The ship returned to Britain in 1824 and was then used as a base for anti-smuggling operations at [[Newhaven]] from 1825 to 1831 before being [[ship breaking|broken up]] in 1833.

==Design and construction==
''Hyperion'' was an [[18-pounder long gun|18-pounder]] [[fifth-rate]] [[frigate]].{{efn|Ships of the Royal Navy were categorised in a rating system. Fifth-rate ships were those holding between thirty and forty-four guns, and usually frigates. They were smaller than fourth-rates, of fifty and sixty guns, but larger than sixth-rates, of twenty to thirty guns.{{sfnp|Lavery|1989|p=40}}}}{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}} Frigates were three-masted, [[full-rigged ship]]s that carried their [[main battery]] on a single, continuous [[gun deck]]. They were smaller and faster than [[ships of the line]] and primarily intended for raiding, reconnaissance and messaging.<ref>{{cite web | year =2022|url=https://www.britannica.com/technology/frigate | title =Frigate | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220712182141/https://www.britannica.com/technology/frigate |archive-date=12 July 2022| publisher =Encyclopaedia Britannica Online | accessdate =18 September 2022}}</ref>{{sfnp|Gardiner|1999|p=56}} During the [[Napoleonic Wars]] the [[Royal Navy]] mostly produced frigates from three well-developed designs.{{efn|The 38-gun [[Leda-class frigate|''Leda'']] and [[Lively-class frigate|''Lively'' classes]] and 36-gun [[Apollo-class frigate|''Apollo'' class]].{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=157}}{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=162}}{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=159}}}} In 1805 the [[First Lord of the Admiralty]], [[Charles Middleton, 1st Baron Barham|Lord Barham]], briefly deviated from this to order three unique vessels each copied from French designs.{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=157}} ''Hyperion'' was one of these frigates; designed by the [[Surveyor of the Navy]], [[John Henslow (Surveyor of the Navy)|Sir John Henslow]], the ship was based on the lines of the 32-gun frigate [[French frigate Magicienne (1778)|HMS ''Magicienne'']], which had been captured from the French in 1781.{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}} Despite her age ''Magicienne'' was a popular vessel, although she was smaller than most modern frigates.{{sfnp|Gardiner|2000|p=17}}

The frigate was ordered on 13 June 1805 to be built at [[Kingston upon Hull]] by William Gibson.{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}} He was a private shipbuilder who had built a new yard on the [[River Hull]] that year to compete for Royal Navy contracts; ''Hyperion'' was the largest ship he constructed.{{sfnp|Knight|Wilcox|2012|p=174}} The ship was [[Keel laying|laid down]] in February 1806 and [[ceremonial ship launching|launched]] on 3 November 1807. ''Hyperion'' had the following dimensions: {{convert|143|ft|9|in|m|1}} along the gun deck, {{convert|118|ft|7+5/8|in|m|1}} at the [[keel#structural keels|keel]], with a [[beam (nautical)|beam]] of {{convert|39|ft|4+1/2|in|m|0}} and a depth in the [[hold (compartment)|hold]] of {{convert|12|ft|4|in|m|1}}. Her [[Draft (hull)|draught]] was {{convert|10|ft|m|0}} forward and {{convert|13|ft|6|in|m|1}} aft, and the ship was calculated at 978 {{small|{{Fraction|34|94}}}} [[tons burthen]]. ''Hyperion'' was [[fitting out|fitted out]] at [[Chatham Dockyard]] between 9 December and 23 April 1808, having cost £20,165 to complete ({{Inflation|index=UK|value=20,165|start_year=1808|end_year=2023|r=0|fmt=eq|cursign=£}}).{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}} She was named ''Hyperion'' after the mythical [[Titans|Titan]] [[Hyperion (Titan)|Hyperion]], the first Royal Navy warship to bear the name.{{sfnp|Manning|Walker|1959|p=238}}

''Hyperion'' had a complement of 254. The ship held twenty-six [[18-pounder long gun|18-pounder]] [[long gun]]s on her upper deck, supported by two 9-pounder long guns and ten 24-pounder [[carronade]]s on her [[quarterdeck]] and a further four 9-pounders and two 24-pounder carronades on her [[forecastle]].{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}}

===Characteristics===
The design mirrored many of the characteristics of the original French ''Magicienne'' plan. ''Hyperion'' had a shallow [[Hull (watercraft)|hull]] which produced a fast ship but left her liable to drift to [[leeward]] and resulted in the ship becoming excessively damp when sailing in rough seas, as the [[gun port]]s were only {{convert|5|ft|11|in|m|1}} above the water.{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}}{{sfnp|Gardiner|2000|p=19}} She also struggled to stow a full set of provisions because of the smaller hold the design created, with most available space taken up by the [[Magazine (artillery)|magazine]].{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}}{{sfnp|Gardiner|2000|p=16}}

Headroom was confined below decks, and officers who served in ''Hyperion'' complained that they were not able to stand up straight in the [[wardroom]].{{sfnp|Gardiner|2000|p=16}} ''Hyperion'' proved to be faster than the other 32-gun frigate based on a French design, [[HMS Bucephalus (1808)|HMS ''Bucephalus'']], and was in fact larger than the third ship produced, [[HMS Pyramus (1810)|HMS ''Pyramus'']], despite her being a 36-gun frigate.{{sfnp|Gardiner|2000|p=19}} The ship sailed and handled well even in the heaviest of conditions, and usually reached between {{convert|12|kn|kph|1}} and {{convert|12.5|kn|kph|1}}, with her top recorded speed at {{convert|13.5|kn|kph|1}}.{{efn|In comparison ''Pyramus'' reached {{convert|13|kn|kph|0}} and ''Bucephalus'' {{convert|10|kn|kph|1}}.{{sfnp|Gardiner|2000|p=143}}}}{{sfnp|Gardiner|2000|p=143}}

==Service==
===Mediterranean===
[[File:John Wood (1801-1870) - Admiral Sir Philip Charles Henderson Calderwood Durham (1763–1845) - PG 2880 - National Galleries of Scotland.jpg|[[Philip Charles Durham]] travelled in ''Hyperion'' during her first commission|thumb|alt=Portrait of a white male with grey hair wearing a blue naval uniform with medals and a red sash; he holds a telescope in his right hand and has a sword on his left hip]]
''Hyperion'' was [[ship commissioning|commissioned]] by [[Captain (Royal Navy)|Captain]] [[Thomas Brodie (Royal Navy officer)|Thomas Brodie]] in January 1808. The frigate sailed to serve in the [[Mediterranean Sea]] on 29 June,{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}} and in early July passed through [[Admiral (Royal Navy)|Admiral]] [[Sir Charles Cotton, 5th Baronet|Sir Charles Cotton]]'s squadron off the [[River Tagus]], giving him the news that [[Lieutenant general (United Kingdom)|Lieutenant-General]] [[Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of Wellington|Sir Arthur Wellesley]] was shortly to land in the [[Iberian Peninsula]] to begin the [[Peninsular War]].{{sfnp|Krajeski|2000|p=85}}

Travelling on board ''Hyperion'' were Captain [[Philip Charles Durham]], joining his ship at [[Minorca]], and the politicians [[Robert Adair (politician)|Robert Adair]] and [[Stratford Canning, 1st Viscount Stratford de Redcliffe|Stratford Canning]], destined for [[Constantinople]]. While sailing off Portugal Brodie, an inexperienced sailor, ordered ''Hyperion'' to prepare for action upon sighting what he thought were enemy warships. Durham informed him he was preparing to attack the [[Berlengas]], rather than any ships.{{sfnp|Rubinstein|2005|p=222}} A few days later ''Hyperion'' narrowly avoided wrecking at [[Sanlúcar de Barrameda]].{{sfnp|Rubinstein|2005|p=223}} Brodie became a figure of ridicule on board when he ordered the ship's geese be killed to ensure their cackling did not give them away to enemy ships.{{sfnp|Rubinstein|2005|p=222}} As the journey continued Adair and Canning lost confidence in Brodie, and when the ship reached [[Palermo]] they went ashore and convinced Captain [[John Stewart (Royal Navy officer)|John Stewart]] of the 38-gun frigate [[HMS Seahorse (1794)|HMS ''Seahorse'']] to take them the rest of the way.{{sfnp|Rubinstein|2005|p=223}}

On 3 August 1809 ''Hyperion'' sailed from [[Gibraltar]] conveying the poet [[Lord Byron]] to [[Cadiz]], arriving there on the following day.{{sfnp|Thomas|1983|p=56}}{{sfnp|Thomas|1983|p=84}} The journey provided inspiration for a similar journey in Byron's ''[[Don Juan (poem)|Don Juan]]''.{{sfnp|Thomas|1983|p=56}}

===Jamaica and Haiti===

Having continued to serve in the Mediterranean, ''Hyperion'' moved to the [[Jamaica Station (Royal Navy)|Jamaica Station]] on 14 January 1810.{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}} At the time, [[Haiti]] was split into two rival governments: the [[Republic of Haiti (1806–1820)|Republic of Haiti]], led by [[Alexandre Pétion]], and the [[State of Haiti]], ruled by [[Henri Christophe]], with the two engaging in a [[low-intensity conflict]].{{sfnp|Premdas|1993|p=88}} The small [[Haitian Coast Guard|Haitian Navy]], loyal to Christophe, blockaded ports held by Pétion and in May captured the British merchantman ''Crown'' as she attempted to enter [[Môle-Saint-Nicolas]]. The Haitians took ''Crown'' to [[Port-de-Paix]] to offload her cargo, and while making the journey the ship was stopped by ''Hyperion''. Brodie boarded the captured vessel but chose not to intervene. The owners of ''Crown'' complained to the commander of the Jamaica Station, [[Vice admiral (Royal Navy)|Vice-Admiral]] [[Bartholomew Rowley]], that ''Hyperion'' was at fault for their loss of {{Inflation|US|60,000|1810|fmt=eq|orig=yes}} worth of goods. Rowley sided with Brodie's opinion that the ship had been lawfully seized, but promised to send an officer to request restitution.{{sfnp|Cole|1967|p=177}}

''Hyperion''{{'}}s involvement with Haiti continued into the following year. In late January 1811, she arrived at [[Gonaïves]] for supplies. There, a British merchant named Richard Simpson smuggled a letter on board explaining that he had been detained by the Haitians while travelling to [[Jérémie]], and was being held under guard at Gonaïves by another British merchant, Matthew O'Brien.{{sfnp|Cole|1967|pp=184–185}}{{sfnp|Hore|2015|pp=61–62}} Brodie quietly brought Simpson on board ''Hyperion'', but on 1 February the Haitian commandant at Gonaïves demanded his return. Brodie spoke with O'Brien and other British merchants ashore, who were fearful of reprisals if Simpson did not come back. Upon hearing this Simpson voluntarily returned to custody and Brodie stayed ashore, dining with the merchant community. On 2 February a [[Launch (boat)|launch]] on a supply run from ''Hyperion'' was fired on by [[Fort-Liberté|Fort Castries]], killing three seamen. Under orders to fire on any boat after Simpson's failed escape, the battery's commander was arrested by Haitian authorities. There are different accounts of what happened after this. The historian Hubert Cole reports that Brodie peacefully accepted a [[military funeral]] for the dead sailors and left Gonaïves, arriving at [[Port Royal]] on 17 February.{{sfnp|Cole|1967|p=185}}

The historian [[Peter Hore (historian)|Peter Hore]] provides a different series of events. He describes the attack on the launch as the prelude to Brodie and two other officers being detained ashore by the Haitians. In this account Brodie was only able to return to ''Hyperion'' after the ship's [[first lieutenant#Royal Navy|first lieutenant]], [[Lieutenant (Royal Navy)|Lieutenant]] [[James Morgan (Royal Navy officer)|James Morgan]], brought the frigate through a channel only {{convert|4|fathom|m}} deep, and sent an ultimatum to the Haitians that if the officers were not released he would destroy the town and capture the Haitian frigate anchored there.{{sfnp|Hore|2015|pp=61–62}}

Upon returning to Jamaica, Brodie reported the incident at Gonaïves to Rowley, who reprimanded him for not demanding a written apology for the killings.{{sfnp|Cole|1967|p=185}} Captain [[James Giles Vashon|James Vashon]] in the 36-gun frigate [[HMS Unicorn (1782)|HMS ''Thalia'']] was sent to rectify this, but the Haitians defended their conduct and Christophe, insulted by Vashon's behaviour, unsuccessfully attempted to incite a [[duel]] with him.{{sfnp|Cole|1967|pp=186–188}} Brodie remained with ''Hyperion'' at Jamaica where, on 14 March, he died of an illness probably caught during his time on Haiti.{{sfnp|Hore|2015|pp=61–62}} He was replaced on 26 March by Captain [[William Pryce Cumby]] who, after the death of Rowley in October, sailed ''Hyperion'' to [[Portsmouth Dockyard]] for a refit, stopping at [[Veracruz (city)|Veracruz]] and [[Havana]].{{sfnp|Tracy|2006|p=104}}{{sfnp|Marshall|1825a|p=971}}

===North America===
''Hyperion'' sailed to join the [[Newfoundland Station]] on 13 May 1812, protecting the whale fisheries amongst icebergs in the [[Davis Strait]].{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}}{{sfnp|Tracy|2006|p=104}}{{sfnp|Marshall|1825a|p=971}} After a summer of this duty, and with the [[War of 1812]] ongoing, the ship served as a convoy escort between Newfoundland and [[Barbados]]. Returning to harbour at [[St. John's, Newfoundland and Labrador|St. John's]], ''Hyperion'' spent the winter frozen there.{{sfnp|Marshall|1825a|p=971}} By April 1813 the ship was struggling with [[desertion]], but Cumby failed in a petition to begin a [[press gang]] on land.{{sfnp|Mercer|2006|pp=279–280}}

The ship left Newfoundland on 10 November as escort to a convoy of thirty-seven ships destined for Portugal. Hit by severe storms, by 25 November only sixteen ships remained in company.{{sfnp|Hall|2004|p=122}} Splitting the convoy, ''Hyperion'' took six ships towards [[Porto]]. With the weather too poor to enter the harbour, they redirected to [[Corunna]] and were beset by another storm. By 6 December ''Hyperion'' had lost the entire convoy. With her upper works damaged and both pumps broken, Cumby diverted the frigate towards Portsmouth, meeting with another battered convoy under the escort of the 44-gun frigate [[HMS Iris (1807)|HMS ''Iris'']].{{sfnp|Hall|2004|p=123}}

===Home service===
[[File:HMS Hyperion captures Rattlesnake 1814 (cropped).jpg|thumb|''Hyperion'' captures ''Rattlesnake'' on 3 June 1814|alt=Drawing showing Hyperion at sea chasing the brig Rattlesnake; Hyperion is trailing behind Rattlesnake and the two ships are firing cannon at each other]]

''Hyperion'' continued on in Europe, patrolling the [[English Channel]] and [[Bay of Biscay]]. In the latter location she captured the American 16-gun [[privateer]] [[brig]] ''Rattlesnake'' on 3 June 1814.{{sfnp|Tracy|2006|p=104}}{{sfnp|Marshall|1825a|p=971}} Cumby was replaced in command temporarily by Captain [[James Lillicrap]] on 24 January the following year, who sailed the frigate to [[Lisbon]] before escorting a convoy from Porto.{{sfnp|O'Byrne|1849a|p=660}} With the wars over, the ship was [[paid off]] on 31 August and [[laid up]] at Portsmouth.{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}}{{sfnp|Tracy|2006|p=104}}

Between June and November 1818 ''Hyperion'' was repaired and prepared for sea at Portsmouth.{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}} As part of this her water storage, usually wooden [[cask]]s, was replaced with iron tanks, increasing the dangerously low distance from gun port to waterline by {{convert|3|in|m|1}}.{{sfnp|Gardiner|2000|p=19}}{{sfnp|Gardiner|2000|p=104}} While this work was underway she was recommissioned by Captain [[Thomas Searle]] on 1 September.{{sfnp|O'Byrne|1849c|p=1047}} The ship initially served at Portsmouth in attendance on [[George IV|George, Prince Regent]], when he sailed off the coast there.{{sfnp|O'Byrne|1849c|p=1047}}{{sfnp|Laughton|Lambert|2004}} The poet [[John Keats]] and his friend [[Charles Armitage Brown]] watched ''Hyperion'' on 12 August 1819 as she sailed off [[Cowes]] alongside [[HMY Royal George|HMY ''Royal George'']], describing the scene as "silent, light and graceful".{{sfnp|Roe|2013|p=337}}

===South America===
''Hyperion'' was subsequently sent to the [[South America Station]].{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}} With the [[Chilean War of Independence]] underway, the frigate was assigned to escort merchant ships, as while [[Viceroyalty of Peru|Spanish Peru]] allowed neutral transport, Chile would seize such vessels in Peruvian waters.{{sfnp|Vale|2004|p=114}} ''Hyperion'' arrived off the Chilean port of [[Valparaiso]] in February 1820. [[Thomas Cochrane, 10th Earl of Dundonald|Lord Cochrane]], fighting for the [[Chilean Navy]], had embargoed three British merchantmen there to avoid word of [[Liberating Expedition of Peru|planned attacks on Peru]] being revealed. Searle obtained permission from [[Bernardo O'Higgins]], [[Supreme Director of Chile]], for the ships to be released, but Cochrane was not made aware and refused to allow it.{{sfnp|Ortiz-Sotelo|1996|p=65}} O'Higgins reiterated his permission on 25 April but by 5 May two of the merchant ships remained at Valparaiso.{{sfnp|Ortiz-Sotelo|1996|pp=65–66}} With Cochrane still not permitting the vessels to leave, on 7 May Searle ordered one, ''Inspector'',  to weigh anchor under the protection of ''Hyperion''. The frigate sailed with her gun ports open and [[broadside (naval)|broadside]] ready, and Cochrane chose not to resist the departure.{{sfnp|Ortiz-Sotelo|1996|p=66}} [[Commodore (Royal Navy)|Commodore]] [[Sir Thomas Hardy, 1st Baronet|Sir Thomas Hardy]], commanding the station, received an official complaint from the Chilean government, and personally disapproved of the action. Over the following months the relationship between Searle and Cochrane deteriorated further when the Chileans detained another British merchant ship.{{sfnp|Ortiz-Sotelo|1996|p=66}}

[[File:Thomas Cochrane, 10th Earl of Dundonald.jpg|The bitter relationship of ''Hyperion''{{'}}s captain with [[Thomas Cochrane, 10th Earl of Dundonald|Lord Cochrane]] resulted in the frigate being withdrawn from South America|thumb|alt=A black and white engraving of a portrait of a white male wearing a naval uniform with a dark top and white trousers; he holds a telescope in both hands]]

With Searle as acting senior officer of the [[British Pacific Squadron]], on 14 April ''Hyperion'' received the first news from the explorer [[William Smith (mariner)|William Smith]] in the brig [[William (1811 Blythe ship)|''William'']] that he had discovered the [[South Shetland Islands]]. Searle ordered the crew of ''William'' not to go ashore at Valparaiso to prevent the news from spreading, and forwarded it to the [[Admiralty (United Kingdom)|Admiralty]]. This was standard procedure for the handling of expedition findings, ensuring that any political or economical advantage from the discovery was not squandered.{{sfnp|Stewart|2014|p=15}} ''Hyperion'' was stationed off the Peruvian port of [[Callao]] by October. Towards the end of the month ''Hyperion'' met with Cochrane again there, with the latter in the 50-gun frigate [[Chilean frigate O'Higgins|''O'Higgins'']]. Upon seeing Cochrane, Searle had ''Hyperion'' loose her guns, causing Cochrane to infer that Searle thought him to be a pirate and was prepared to attack him as such.{{sfnp|Ortiz-Sotelo|1996|p=66}}

''Hyperion'' was still off Callao on 5 November, alongside the American 38-gun frigate [[HMS Macedonian|USS ''Macedonian'']], when Cochrane [[Naval boarding|cut out]] the Spanish 40-gun frigate [[Spanish frigate Esmeralda|''Esmeralda'']] there. Searle observed strict neutrality during the fight in the port, not acknowledging the attackers as their boats went past the ship, while the crew of ''Macedonian'' cheered Cochrane's force.{{efn|The sentries on ''Hyperion'' loudly called their challenges to the attacking boats as they attempted to pass by in secrecy, and Searle threatened to arrest one of his [[midshipmen]] who started cheering when ''Esmeralda'' was boarded.{{sfnp|Thomas|2002|p=266}}{{sfnp|Thomas|2002|p=269}}}}{{sfnp|Thomas|2002|p=266}}{{sfnp|Vale|2004|pp=114–116}} As the captured frigate sailed out, Cochrane had ''Esmeralda'' copy the pattern of lights being hoisted by ''Hyperion'' and ''Macedonian'' in order to confuse the defenders, who he knew would want to avoid hitting a neutral vessel.{{sfnp|Thomas|2002|p=268}} The attack was completed in half an hour, and Searle had to quickly move ''Hyperion'' out of the way to avoid Spanish return fire, with the ship being hit several times. Despite his neutral posture, he reported the action as a "most brilliant affair".{{sfnp|Vale|2004|p=116}}{{sfnp|Ortiz-Sotelo|1996|p=67}} The population of Callao believed the two neutral ships must have colluded in the attack, and when a boat from ''Macedonian'' landed on the following day, it was attacked, and two men were killed. A boat from ''Hyperion'' intervened to stop further damage.{{sfnp|Ortiz-Sotelo|1996|p=68}}

Searle's relationship with the Chileans did not thaw after this. When the British merchantman ''Pacific'' arrived at Callao on 21 November, an officer was sent from ''O'Higgins'' with a communique about the vessel. Searle refused to receive the letter, throwing it overboard, ostensibly to retain his neutrality.{{sfnp|Ortiz-Sotelo|1996|p=66}} His behaviour was so diplomatically egregious that the Admiralty had to take action, noting that he had been{{blockquote|"Intemperate and injudicious; and as repetition of it might, and probably would, be attended with consequences highly injurious to the public interest, their Lordships direct that ''Hyperion'' be removed from the South American station".{{sfnp|Ortiz-Sotelo|1996|p=67}}}} 

''Hyperion'' took on board [[Bullion coin|specie]] worth {{Inflation|US|1,500,000|1818|fmt=eq|orig=yes|r=-3}} from British merchants at Callao, and then sailed for Britain on 10 December, also conveying the artist [[Augustus Earle]]. Some of this specie was actually fraudulent, being lead inside the sealed boxes rather than silver.{{sfnp|Ortiz-Sotelo|1996|p=247}}{{sfnp|Gough|1983|p=421}} This was the first major shipment of specie from South America to be protected privately for a profit by a warship, in a system that would continue for the next century.{{sfnp|Gough|1983|pp=420–421}}{{sfnp|Smith|2006}}

===Cape of Good Hope and anti-piracy===
[[File:Augustus Earle (1793-1838) - Divine Service as it is Usually Performed on Board a British Frigate at Sea - BHC1119 - Royal Museums Greenwich.jpg|Divine service on the [[gun deck]] of a frigate, by [[Augustus Earle]], based on a service on ''Hyperion''{{sfnp|Lambert|2000|p=44}}|thumb|alt=A painting showing the lower deck of a frigate; crew members wearing either blue or red uniforms are sat around the open space, with the captain sat at a table in the centre reading from a book]]

The ship was paid off in April 1821 but recommissioned in the same month, with Lillicrap again in command from 6 April.{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}}{{sfnp|O'Byrne|1849a|p=660}} Sent to serve on the [[Cape of Good Hope Station]], ''Hyperion'' sailed from [[Plymouth]] in September with the [[List of governors of British South African colonies|governor of the Cape Colony]], [[Lord Charles Somerset]], on board.{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}}{{sfnp|Millar|1965|p=134}} The ship arrived in [[Table Bay]] on 30 November.{{sfnp|Millar|1965|p=135}} Lillicrap assumed command of the station as a commodore, with ''Hyperion'' as his [[flagship]]. On 10 June 1822 the squadron saved the [[East Indiaman]] ''Albion'' from wrecking in [[Simon's Bay]] during a storm, and also rescued two Russian warships.{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}}{{sfnp|O'Byrne|1849a|p=660}}

In the same year Lillicrap was superseded as commodore, and ''Hyperion'' sailed to the [[West Indies]] to again join the Jamaica Station.{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}}{{sfnp|Marshall|1825b|p=880}} With piracy rife off [[Cuba]], [[Rear admiral (Royal Navy)|Rear-Admiral]] [[Sir Charles Rowley, 1st Baronet|Sir Charles Rowley]] sent ''Hyperion'' there on 6 October to co-ordinate with the [[governor of Cuba]], [[Sebastián Kindelán]].{{sfnp|McCarthy|2013|p=146}} Lillicrap had orders to violate Spanish territory if Kindelán refused to act against the pirates, but the Spaniard was supportive, providing Lillicrap with letters of authority to assist in combatting the pirate base at [[Bahía Honda, Cuba|Bahía Honda]].{{sfnp|McCarthy|2013|pp=146–147}} Lillicrap was moved to the 74-gun [[ship of the line]] [[HMS Gloucester (1812)|HMS ''Gloucester'']] on 24 October 1823 and replaced by Captain [[George Rich (Royal Navy officer)|George Rich]].{{sfnp|O'Byrne|1849a|p=660}}{{sfnp|O'Byrne|1849b|p=972}} ''Hyperion'' continued in the West Indies until 15 May 1824 when she sailed from Port Royal to return to Britain, having been deemed unfit for further service. She arrived at Portsmouth on 19 June and then sailed to [[Woolwich Dockyard]] to be paid off.<ref>{{cite news|date=1 July 1824|page=4|title=Naval Intelligence|work=The Caledonian Mercury|location=Edinburgh|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image-view/393186000}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|date=28 June 1824|page=4|title=Portsmouth|work=Hampshire Telegraph and Naval Chronicle|location=Portsmouth|url=https://www.newspapers.com/image-view/390156020}}</ref>

===Final years===
''Hyperion'' was fitted for anti-smuggling duties at [[Sheerness Dockyard]] between January and March 1825. Recommissioned in the same January, she served at [[Newhaven]] under Captain [[William Mingaye]].{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}} The majority of the crew was sent ashore to serve in [[Martello tower]]s, while ''Hyperion'' stayed at the port alongside two [[Ship's tender|tenders]], [[HMS Highflyer (1822)|HMS ''Highflyer'']] and [[HMS Viper (1820)|HMS ''Viper'']].{{sfnp|Bowen|1924|p=278}}{{sfnp|Larkin|2008|pp=76-78}} The frigate continued at Newhaven until she was paid off for a final time in May 1831, and was [[ship breaking|broken up]] at Portsmouth in June 1833.{{sfnp|Winfield|2008|p=165}}

